<div id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we assess the effectiveness of GDE. The baseline comparison employs GFS-e, which preserves the same SQA and PTS as GFS but replaces the GPU demand prediction algorithm in GDE with a method that takes the peak GPU demand of tenants from the previous week for future predictions. This was a naive and conservative approach adopted in the production cluster. Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.6.1. GPU Demand Estimator ‣ 4.6. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Performance Evaluation ‣ GFS: A Preemption-aware Scheduling Framework for GPU Clusters with Predictive Spot Instance Management" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the ablation results of GDE. Although GDE has a minimal effect on SLO compliance for HP tasks, it significantly improves SLO for spot tasks, decreasing JCT, JQT, and <math id="S4.SS6.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> by 48.20%, 94.52%, and 85.02%, respectively, compared to the baseline. These metrics underscore GDE’s critical role in enhancing SLO guarantees for spot tasks and scheduling efficiency in GFS.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.6.2. </span>Spot Quota Allocator</h4>

<figure id="S4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 9</span>. </span><span id="S4.T9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">The ablation experimental results of SQA</span></figcaption>
